------------------------------------ Reactive user score -------------------------------------------

- OUR USER SCORE SHOULD UPDATE IN REAL TIME. ANOTHER USE-CASE FOR OBSERVABLES AND SIGNALS!

- We now have a nice WebSocket service, and we are going to use it again to have real time updates
    on the user’s score.

- ✅Indeed, the score displayed on the right of the menu is not really accurate. Every time we bet
    on a pony, we lose a bit of money, and every time we win a bet we earn some.
    It would be really neat if the "money" counter was updated in real time


1️⃣ To do so, the backend offers an API to receive the player data
    (https://ponyracer.ninja-squad.com/apidoc#_getting_score_updates), on the WebSocket channel
    /player/{myUserId}. Create a new method in UserService called scoreUpdates, that accepts a
    userId, uses our WsService to connect to this API and returns the Observable created.
    user-service.ts:

        scoreUpdates(userId: number): Observable<UserModel> {
            return this.wsService.connect<UserModel>(`/player/${userId}`);
        }



2️⃣ Now, in our Menu, let’s update the initialization logic.
    Until now, we were using currentUser directly. 🔥Now we need to chain this with a call to our
    new scoreUpdates method, to subscribe to the score updates. ⚠️Note that the object emitted when
    the score changes is a UserModel, like the value stored in the currentUser signal when a user
    logs in.
    🧠To do so, initialize the user field with rxResource. 🔥🔥The params function should return
    this.userService.currentUser(). 🔥🔥The stream function then receives the current user in params.
    🔥🔥If we have a user, return the Observable created by the call to scoreUpdates(user.id). If we
    don’t have a user, we don’t want to call the API, so we’ll return of(undefined).
    menu.ts:

        protected readonly user = rxResource({
            params: () => this.userService.currentUser(),
            stream: ({ params: user }) => {
                return user ? this.userService.scoreUpdates(user.id) : of(undefined)
            }
        })
    
    Looks good, but the user will now only be received when the score of the user changes. We also
    want to receive the user as it is now.

        time                       1sec                           2sec
    ------U--------------------------------------------------------------|- currentUser

    rxResource({ params: () => currentUser(), 
    stream: ({ params: user }) => user ? /* scoreUpdates */ : of(undefined) })

    -------------------------------U1--------------------U2--------------|- scoreUpdates


    What we want is an Observable that looks like:

        time                       1sec                           2sec
    ------U--------------------------------------------------------------|- currentUser

    rxResource({ params: () => currentUser(), stream: ({ params: user }) => user ?
        /* scoreUpdates */.pipe(startWith(user)) :
        of(undefined)
    })

    ------U------------------------U1--------------------U2--------------|- scoreUpdates


    We need to start the score updates observable with the current user: that’s where the RxJS
    startWith operator comes handy.
    And Last thing: ⚠️⚠️the score updates can potentially throw an error (the network connection
    may break), and if that happens, the whole observable will stop! That means the user would never
    change again if a score update fails. To fix this, you need to add a catchError operator that
    returns an empty observable if an error arises:

        protected readonly user = rxResource({
            params: () => this.userService.currentUser(),
            stream: ({ params: user }) => {
                return user ? this.userService.scoreUpdates(user.id)
                .pipe(startWith(user), catchError(() => EMPTY)) :
                of(undefined)
            }
        })
        


3️⃣ Previously user was a signal. Now After the step 2️⃣, it is a rxResource so update the template
    accordingly with user.value().


